WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.048
On February 28th, 2022 a Russian attack 

00:00:04.048 --> 00:00:07.707
hit the Ukrainian city of Kharkiv 
in a residential area.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:11.559
Four people died collecting water from a water
point in that neighborhood

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:13.478
and many more were injured.

00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:18.811
We had lots of videos from social media that were
documenting the attack from multiple angles.

00:00:18.811 --> 00:00:22.904
Some videos taken from afar, 
from high rise buildings...

00:00:22.904 --> 00:00:27.670
Milena Marin and her team at Amnesty International
gather evidence like this

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.903
looking for signs of war crimes.

00:00:29.903 --> 00:00:33.132
We want to see was that, in any way, 
a legitimate strike.

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:38.210
And among the videos and photos they found 
showing the destruction of civilian property

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.187
one particular piece of evidence 
caught their attention.

00:00:41.187 --> 00:00:43.351
It’s a remnant from a cluster bomb

00:00:43.351 --> 00:00:46.800
a weapon so destructive to civilians that
its use alone

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.340
can constitute a war crime.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.453
So why do the world’s most powerful militaries
still use them?

00:00:57.107 --> 00:00:59.176
Cluster munition strikes involve rockets

00:00:59.176 --> 00:01:02.876
that contain dozens or even hundreds of smaller
cluster bombs.

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:06.907
This is the nose cone of a rocket from another
attack in Kharkiv

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:08.434
and this is the tail.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:12.838
The image Milena analyzed is one of the smaller
cluster bombs.

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:15.177
When the rocket releases cluster bombs

00:01:15.177 --> 00:01:18.455
they scatter over an area as large as several
city blocks

00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:20.802
causing widespread explosions.

00:01:20.802 --> 00:01:25.748
Like this video verified by Bellingcat
taken from another attack in Kharkiv.

00:01:25.748 --> 00:01:28.992
Each circle is one of the cluster bombs exploding.

00:01:28.992 --> 00:01:31.659
It’s this imprecision that makes cluster
bombs illegal

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:34.097
under the rules of war, in most cases.

00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:38.407
Those rules were created by a series of international
treaties that span decades.

00:01:38.407 --> 00:01:40.416
They govern how wars should be fought.

00:01:40.416 --> 00:01:44.021
They prohibit things like intentionally targeting
civilians.

00:01:44.021 --> 00:01:47.252
And they ban what’s known as 
"indiscriminate attacks".

00:01:47.252 --> 00:01:50.119
Every state military has the obligation

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:55.151
to use as precise weapons as possible to hit
military targets and not civilian targets.

00:01:55.151 --> 00:01:59.339
A precise attack on a military target like
a base or weapons hanger

00:01:59.339 --> 00:02:00.969
is permissible under the law

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:05.000
because it intentionally avoids civilian areas.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.523
In an area with both military and civilian
structures,

00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:12.790
militaries have to be careful to only target
non-civilian areas.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:17.546
Striking a populated, mainly-residential area,
like the attack in Kharkiv

00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:20.213
is almost impossible to justify legally.

00:02:20.213 --> 00:02:23.046
Especially if you are using cluster munitions.

00:02:23.046 --> 00:02:27.418
They’re extremely bad at targeting and releasing
them anywhere near civilians

00:02:27.418 --> 00:02:31.035
results in indiscriminate damage.

00:02:31.035 --> 00:02:36.618
Of the roughly 23,000 documented cluster munitions
deaths worldwide since the 60s

00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:40.093
about 94% have been civilians.

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:43.389
Some of that is because of the high failure
rate of the smaller bombs

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:46.505
up to 30% of them don’t explode on impact.

00:02:46.505 --> 00:02:49.726
So they remain on the ground, posing a risk
indefinitely.

00:02:49.726 --> 00:02:53.288
A vast majority of cluster munition deaths,
80%

00:02:53.288 --> 00:02:56.904
are from these lingering remnants as opposed
to the initial strike.

00:02:56.904 --> 00:03:01.702
These weapons sit for decades and decades
and are just as deadly and capable

00:03:01.702 --> 00:03:06.199
when a farmer hits them with a plow 
in 1970 or in 2070.

00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:07.983
They're still going to kill and injure.

00:03:08.419 --> 00:03:11.432
The US dropped millions of cluster bombs in
Southeast Asia

00:03:11.432 --> 00:03:14.143
in the 60s and 70s during the Vietnam War.

00:03:14.143 --> 00:03:18.511
Those same bombs still kill and injure hundreds
of people to this day.

00:03:18.511 --> 00:03:21.420
Including in March of this year, when a man
in Laos was killed

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.466
by a US bomb he encountered while making a
fire to cook.

00:03:25.466 --> 00:03:28.714
These small, metallic remnants are often brightly
colored

00:03:28.714 --> 00:03:31.681
which is why children are attracted to them.

00:03:31.681 --> 00:03:34.715
In Kosovo, for example, after 
the Yugoslav Wars

00:03:34.715 --> 00:03:40.079
62.5% of casualties from cluster bombs were
boys under the age of 18.

00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:46.062
When Milena’s team at Amnesty International’s
Crisis Evidence Lab got reports

00:03:46.062 --> 00:03:48.004
 of the February 28th attack

00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:51.603
reports of cluster bombs were just one element
they began verifying.

00:03:51.603 --> 00:03:55.564
My team is cataloging most of the videos
that are coming from Ukraine

00:03:55.564 --> 00:03:58.984
focusing on those that can qualify
as human rights violations

00:03:58.984 --> 00:04:00.975
under definitions of war crimes.

00:04:00.975 --> 00:04:05.000
It’s illegal to target civilians or to use
indiscriminate weapons.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.426
We do a lot of research to see whether there's
weapons, hangars,

00:04:09.426 --> 00:04:11.589
or anything that could justify an attack.

00:04:11.589 --> 00:04:14.647
Google Earth imagery of the area, captured
before the war

00:04:14.647 --> 00:04:17.148
only appears to show civilian structures:

00:04:17.148 --> 00:04:22.300
high-rise apartment buildings, a hotel,
an auto parts store, and a gondola station.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.411
The man who took the cluster bomb photo told
me he heard a mistaken military target

00:04:26.411 --> 00:04:29.285
may have been this recreational shooting range.

00:04:29.285 --> 00:04:33.182
So what we've done was to build a 3D model
of the neighborhoods

00:04:33.182 --> 00:04:37.473
and overlay all of the videos and imagery
we found onto the 3D model

00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:39.607
to really understand the footprint 
of the attack.

00:04:39.607 --> 00:04:44.202
We did that because we thought that 
cluster munitions were used.

00:04:44.202 --> 00:04:46.325
The man who took the bomb photo lives here.

00:04:46.325 --> 00:04:50.454
He found the bomb a few meters from this parking
lot where his car was parked.

00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:53.752
The photo got forwarded to Amnesty International
to analyze.

00:04:53.752 --> 00:04:57.878
The manufacture date was from 2019 and 
Milena’s team determined

00:04:57.878 --> 00:05:01.653
it was a Russian-made 9N235 cluster munition,

00:05:01.653 --> 00:05:05.288
shot from a 300mm Smerch rocket system, 
like this.

00:05:05.288 --> 00:05:09.043
All of this evidence showed that the attack
focused on civilians

00:05:09.043 --> 00:05:13.300
nowhere near a military target, using 
an indiscriminate weapon.

00:05:13.603 --> 00:05:14.840
A potential war crime.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.865
And everything we do is with the purpose 
of justice and accountability.

00:05:18.865 --> 00:05:22.342
Really conclusive evidence, sometimes
tends to come later on.

00:05:22.342 --> 00:05:27.358
The rule of war banning indiscriminate attacks
was created in 1977.

00:05:27.358 --> 00:05:30.000
But it didn’t stop countries from using
cluster munitions

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.600
 in civilian-populated war zones.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.131
Like the Soviets in Afghanistan...

00:05:35.131 --> 00:05:38.418
NATO forces in Kosovo and Yugoslavia...

00:05:38.418 --> 00:05:39.911
Israel in Lebanon...

00:05:39.911 --> 00:05:42.929
and by the US in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:05:42.929 --> 00:05:48.559
The civilian impact of these bombings motivated
a cluster munitions treaty in 2008

00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:50.829
which sought to ban the weapon outright.

00:05:50.829 --> 00:05:56.568
And it included obligations to assist victims
and clean up undetonated remnants.

00:05:57.157 --> 00:05:59.657
It was signed by over 100 countries.

00:05:59.657 --> 00:06:02.581
But, notably, not by the worst offenders.

00:06:02.581 --> 00:06:08.803
“We don’t have countries such as the US,
China and Russia that are major producers,

00:06:08.803 --> 00:06:12.733
and have been users, the US and Russia of
cluster munitions.”

00:06:12.733 --> 00:06:18.226
That means today, these non-signatory countries
still may be producing cluster munitions.

00:06:18.226 --> 00:06:22.563
And these countries still likely stockpile
them, for different reasons.

00:06:22.563 --> 00:06:24.838
The U.S. maintains its arsenal because

00:06:24.838 --> 00:06:28.007
in some theoretical conflict where there's
a number of tanks

00:06:28.007 --> 00:06:30.000
and people outside of those tanks

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.189
if you used cluster munitions on that

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:36.319
you could both damage the vehicles and
injure the people outside of them.

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:39.883
Having a weapon that does that is useful
in a military arsenal.

00:06:39.883 --> 00:06:44.765
However, there's other weapons that do that
that don't produce thousands of duds

00:06:44.765 --> 00:06:47.855
that can affect civilians for a long time
afterwards.

00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:52.474
I asked Brian to compare cluster munitions
to another type of banned warfare:

00:06:52.474 --> 00:06:53.795
chemical weapons.

00:06:53.795 --> 00:06:57.883
The ethical and legal arguments are actually
very similar

00:06:57.883 --> 00:07:02.603
which is the inherently indiscriminate nature
of using either weapons,

00:07:02.603 --> 00:07:05.252
Like cluster munitions, the use of 
chemical weapons

00:07:05.252 --> 00:07:09.307
violates a ban on “indiscriminate attacks”
among other rules of war.

00:07:09.307 --> 00:07:12.748
It’s impossible to use a gaseous weapon
in a precise way.

00:07:12.748 --> 00:07:18.094
After their use in World War I, a Geneva protocol
banned the use of chemical weapons.

00:07:18.094 --> 00:07:22.892
And in 1993, a chemical weapons treaty made
that ban more explicit

00:07:22.892 --> 00:07:27.450
by also prohibiting the development, production,
and stockpiling of the weapon.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.735
Nearly every country signed on to it.

00:07:30.145 --> 00:07:33.783
But that level of committment just hasn’t
happened with cluster bombs

00:07:33.783 --> 00:07:38.693
partly because chemical weapons have been
explicitly banned and stigmatized for so long.

00:07:43.024 --> 00:07:47.751
Since the signing of the 2008 treaty, 
cluster munitions are still in use.

00:07:47.751 --> 00:07:52.995
This container and cluster bomb were used
in the last known US cluster munition strike

00:07:52.995 --> 00:07:55.054
in Yemen in 2009.

00:07:55.054 --> 00:07:59.896
The initial impact killed 41 civilians,
including 23 children.

00:07:59.896 --> 00:08:03.747
Russia and the Syrian government have used
cluster munitions in Syria

00:08:03.747 --> 00:08:07.331
which is why Syria has had the highest rate
of cluster munition casualties 

00:08:07.331 --> 00:08:09.797
since their civil war began.

00:08:09.797 --> 00:08:14.714
And reports suggest Russia has used cluster
bombs at least 24 times in Ukraine...

00:08:14.714 --> 00:08:17.400
a low estimate.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:21.283
I interviewed a man who was wounded by 
cluster munition.

00:08:21.283 --> 00:08:25.288
He still had the pieces because the surgeon
took him out of his body and he kept them.

00:08:25.288 --> 00:08:30.127
One of the pellets that was stuck in his body was
very distinctive as coming from a certain

00:08:30.127 --> 00:08:31.840
kind of Russian submunition.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.717
The mountain of evidence and the number of
indiscriminate attacks is really becoming

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:38.802
quite overwhelming.

00:08:38.802 --> 00:08:43.594
Not just one attack or two attacks or ten
or a hundred, but just continuously.

00:08:43.594 --> 00:08:47.612
Any one of these attacks would be a crime
all on their own.

00:08:47.612 --> 00:08:52.006
Along with the international community, the
US has alleged war crimes in Ukraine.

00:08:52.006 --> 00:08:55.155
But they haven't acknowledged their own role
in perpetuating

00:08:55.155 --> 00:08:57.822
the use of cluster munitions 
around the world.

00:08:57.822 --> 00:09:00.627
This type of hypocrisy shows how the rules
of war

00:09:00.627 --> 00:09:04.006
are only as strong as the worst offenders
allow them to be.

00:09:04.006 --> 00:09:07.348
International justice and accountability is
extremely challenging

00:09:07.348 --> 00:09:09.909
and impunity is one of the reasons why these
weapons

00:09:09.909 --> 00:09:12.070
keep getting used again and again and again.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.716
A potential prosecution for war crimes is why

00:09:14.716 --> 00:09:17.418
human rights investigations are so important.

00:09:17.418 --> 00:09:21.981
But for investigators like Milena, a criminal
trial is just the long game.

00:09:21.981 --> 00:09:27.048
We need to show what's happening to people
trapped between the two armies.

00:09:27.048 --> 00:09:30.225
And that hopefully will sustain the attention
on Ukraine,

00:09:30.225 --> 00:09:32.463
will provide immediate support to the victims

00:09:32.463 --> 00:09:35.148
would provide support to those fleeing the
country.

00:09:35.148 --> 00:09:39.970
While the legal proceedings will last years
those people have immediate needs.

